INT: Could you sell any products in Obava itself?

LR: Very little, because mostly everybody was a farmer. So everybody had a little-- some have more or less, but mostly everybody had enough for their own. So they didn't have to buy.

INT: Aside from the travel and economic hardship, were there any other hardships at the start of the war?

LR: Well, you, you, you couldn't do, you couldn't do things. You have-- you, you, you have to wear Star of David. You couldn't go to the city. The-- what else? That was enough. The Jew couldn't do what somebody else could.

INT: The police, were, were these police--

LR: The police used to beat up Jews already that time. You know, you go to report and come home, and bloody, beaten up for no reason, just because you're a Jew. And there was nothing you could do to report. There is nothing you could do. Already then you had no, no say-so in anything. You didn't had no rights. Your rights was eliminating every day. Was-- they-- there's nothing you, you could say, you could do.

INT: Did they--

LR: Whatever they done to you.

INT: Did they confiscate or take any of your land or crops or cattle at the start of the war?

LR: Yeah. No. Yeah. They, they come. They take it away. They need it for the army. They come and take away and no, no question to ask. You, you couldn't ask no questions.

INT: Now, was the Czech, Czechoslovakian army, were they actively involved in the war in 1939 or 1940?

LR: No. They were not involved. They were very against the war, you know. They don't-- they, they were not involved. They were very-- because they took Sudetenland away from them, so they were not, they were not involved in the war.

INT: But German soldiers were not anywhere near your town?

LR: No, near our town, no. There was no--

INT: The police, what ethnic background were the police?

LR: The police were-- at that time in-- mostly Hungarian.

INT: Now, were there any Ukrainian police too?

LR: No.

INT: Say they were Hungarian ethnics.

LR: Hungarian during that time. When the war started they were Hungarian.

INT: And the Ukrainian population, the ethnic Russians who were Ukrainian, were they subject to hardship as well?

LR: No.

INT: Were any of them beaten or--

LR: No. Just the Jew. Nothing. Not any other nationality. Just the Jews were--

INT: Did you-- was there a sense of cooperation between the Hungarian police and the Ukrainian ethnics?

LR: Yeah. They were, they were-- the only thing they would is, is the Jew they couldn't do to-- couldn't do things, couldn't go places. That was only the Jew. None of the other nationalities were depressed like we were.

INT: In the early years of the war, were you still able to practice your religion?

LR: Well, you practice in your house which-- but not, not, you know go, go to temple. Mostly you, you pray in the house. You were, you were afraid to go to temple. You were afraid that the police might come and beat you up, so mostly you pray in your house.

INT: So you, you never went to synagogue after the start of--

LR: No, after, after things started to get bad we mostly prayed-- we pray every morning, but it was in your own home.

INT: Did your father still actively practice his religion, though?

LR: He did. He [? bathed. ?] Even, even we already-- in-- when they gathered us together, the four weeks that we were before they shipped us to Auschwitz, even there he still prayed every morning. I mean, not tallis and tefillin, but prayed them.

INT: And--

LR: Jews believe all the time in the prayer.

INT: Was he ever actively persecuted for practicing his religion publicly?

LR: No. No.

INT: Did you ever see your father beaten before the war?

LR: No.

INT: And what about your mother?

LR: No.

INT: And your brothers, were they actively practicing their religion as well?

LR: They would, yes.

INT: And did they ever run into trouble after the war started, but before the deportations?

LR: Nothing. Because when they were 18, they were taken away to the labor camp.

INT: Both of your brothers were?

LR: Both of my brothers.

INT: And that was in what year?

LR: That was in [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] '42.

INT: In '42 they were-- and where were they taken?

LR: They were taking to-- they worked behind the army as laborers digging, digging trenches and working behind the army as labor work.

INT: In what--

LR: Behind the Hungarian army.

INT: In what, what part of--

LR: It mostly was in-- what, it was the Carpathian or Hungary.

INT: Did you have contact with your brothers while they were in the labor camps?

LR: No.

INT: Do you remember the day that they were taken away--

LR: Yes.

INT: --to the labor camp?

LR: Yeah.

INT: And what kind of day was that in your house?

LR: Well, I mean, they, they were taken away. We thought they were just taken away for a few days, think they'll come back. But they went for four years.

INT: Do you remember your parents' reaction to them being taken away?

LR: Well, they were very upset. But there's nothing you could say, you could do.

INT: Did--

LR: That-- at that time all the 18, 19-year-old young Jewish boys were taken away as laborers to work behind the army.

INT: Did you have any contact with your brothers at all?

LR: No, nothing. After they were taken away from the house, we didn't had no contact with them. They-- we never had writing from them, anything. I-- they couldn't, they couldn't write. They were not allowed to write.

INT: And did you have any idea where they were taken at that time?

LR: We knew where they were taking to Miskolc, there was a city. It's M-I-S-K-O-LC. That's Carpathian the borders with Hungary. There was a city where they were taking to. I know that they were going at first.

INT: And the Carpathian is a region that borders Hungary. It's in the Czech--

LR: It borders--

INT: --Hungarian border.

LR: It borders Hungary on one side, Poland on the other side, and Slovakia on the-- in the fourth side.

INT: What kind of work where they forced to do?

LR: Labor work, hard labor work. Very little food.

INT: Did they lose a lot of weight while they were working?

LR: That's what we heard. We never had, like I told you, writing from them, or anything. That's why we heard that there was very little food and very hard work.

INT: Your mother and father, how was their health during the time of-- after the war started but prior to deportations? Were they in good health?

LR: They were in good health. Both of them were in good health. They were only in the late 40s.

INT: And what about you?

LR: I was in good health too.

INT: Were you still-- you were actively working on the farm during that period of your life?

LR: [SIGHS] Yes. Yes, just to keep busy.

INT: Were you in good spirits? Did you feel badly about--

LR: No, we-- nobody-- no Jewish person could be at that time in good spirit. We just-- you know, you worked to keep busy, to, to pass the time, that we thought everything would change and turn around for the better.

INT: So in '42, all the young Jewish men in your town--

LR: They were take-- taken to-- as laborers for-- behind the army.

INT: So you had basically women, children, and elderly people or older people left in town.

LR: Older people, right.

INT: And was there any-- was there a lot of communication and, and activity between the remaining Jews in town after that point?

LR: No. There was very little activity, very little you could do. You couldn't go anywhere. You couldn't do anything. You just--

INT: I mean, you wouldn't go to a town's persons house, a landsman's house?

LR: No. Everybody was sitting and waiting for next what's gonna be.

INT: In '41 and '42, did you have an idea of what was going on in the outside world?

LR: We knew that there is a war. We knew there's a war because we had the papers. We knew the war is going on in Russia. We, we knew that Hitler took it, all these countries. We knew everything going on. But we thought that will soon end and nothing would happen.

INT: Was there any special significance to the German invasion of, of Russia? Did that signal a, a different phase of the war to you? Did it mean anything different?

LR: It's-- you know, when, when they attacked the Russian, we thought they, they won't-- they wouldn't last long, that the Russian army, you know, that-- no time will end. But it lasted for so many years.

INT: When did you first start seeing Germans in your region?

LR: I didn't saw Germans in region, just the Hungarian. Germans didn't come to our place, just the Hungarian, because the Hungarian were together with the Germans. The Hungarian army and the Germans, they were all together. So in our place, the Germans didn't come. The Hungarian took us away from the home.

INT: So you were never under German occupation?

LR: When I was in Auschwitz.

INT: Right. But in your-- in Obava.

LR: But not in, not in home. Not in home, no. Not in home. But the, the Hungarian was just as vicious as the Germans. They were-- 'dirty Jew.' And it's--

INT: Were the Hungarian-- Hungarians-- were there any Hungarian Nazis, or--

LR: They were just like the, like the Nazis.

INT: Did they dress in SS uniforms?

LR: They call Nyilas. Nyilas.

INT: How do you spell that?

LR: It's N-I-L-O-S. They used to call it like the Nazis for the Germans, the Hungarian were the Nyilas.

INT: And they were the equivalent of SS men?

LR: Of SS. Was same thing. Was same thing. They were very, very mean, very bad, very--

INT: So--

LR: They, they, they, they saw a Jew, said, oh, it's a shame that I have to waste my bullet on you. I'd rather kill you with a knife than, than waste a bullet on you. You know, they were-- it's a shame you should, you should breathe that good air that we have here. It was-- a Jew was put down. They were just like the Nazis, the Nyilases, under--

INT: And if you said something.

LR: --under-- they were-- it was Hungarian, the [NON-ENGLISH]. I don't know how you, how you say it in English. That there was the, the leader there.

INT: If, if somebody resisted the Hungarians or, or talked back to them, what would the reaction--

LR: No, no, nobody-- nobody would resist it. Nobody resisted. You were beat up for no, no resisting, doing nothing.

INT: So if you ever talked back to one of these men--

LR: No, no-- nobody, nobody did it because you were afraid.

INT: Did they used to round up people in-- before '43 and '42, '41, '42, were there ever roundups of people besides the young men in town?

LR: They used to round up and beat them up, like I said. They used to go report every, every Sunday to the, to the police. And they used to find a Jew on the street and beat him up for no reason, just because you're a Jew, the Hungarian Nyilases.

INT: These Hungarians, were they Czechoslovakian citizens, though?

LR: No. They were Hungarian. They were not Czechoslovakian. They were from Hungary.

INT: They were Hungarian.

LR: Uh-huh.

INT: And they had just come in as part of the German occupation?

LR: Yeah, right. They come to our country. They, they occupy at that time the Carpathian.

INT: So really, the Hungarians--

LR: The--

INT: --were in effect the invaders.

LR: The, the Czech left and the Hungarian invaded. That's when the Carpathian-- so they were the--

INT: Did you ever see any regular army, Hungarian army, or was it all these sort of Hungarian SS men?

LR: No. I saw the Hungarian army. They were at--

INT: Did they have a substantial presence in, in your area?

LR: Yeah. Yeah. They came. When, when the Czech left and they took it over, there was the army there.

INT: What happened to the local government? Was it run by the Hungarians?

LR: By the Hungarian. After they took it over, they run everything by the Hungarian.

INT: And what kind of administration did they have?

LR: They-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I know it was very bad. It was-- they took it over, and for us Jews was very bad. That's all I could remember, that the Jews were very beat up and couldn't go nowhere and no rights.

INT: Did they ever try to house all the Jews of your region of Obava, Munkács, together in a, a ghetto type situation?

LR: Oh, that happened in 1944 when they gathered us together, in April, 1944.

INT: So in '43 you were still living in your farm-- on your farm?

LR: We're still living, yeah, in our home, right.

INT: So really, from the start of the war in September of, of 1939, until--

LR: Till '44 when the, when the Germans retreated from-- you know, from Russia they came, they came to our country. There they, they gathered us and--

INT: Were there any deportations to camps, to concentration camps, before 1944?

LR: Not, not before, just only the young, the young adults, like the 18, 19, 20 years old, they took it as laborers behind the, behind the army.

INT: When the Germans came-- you said the Germans came in '44.

LR: Germans came in '44.

INT: What month in '44?

LR: April.

INT: April of '44. And how did you first notice the fact that there were German soldiers in town?

LR: The Germans didn't come exactly to our village, because the Hungarian Polizei, used to call gendarme, used to call the police, but our police, the Hungarian, used to call gendarme. And they come-- when they come that we should get a few belongings and come which-- with us. That was in April, 1944.

INT: And what did you think at that point?

LR: Well, we knew. We, we heard that's going on there-- in our surrounding villages, that they-- people was gathered and sent to, sent to big factories to stay there. And we knew that's going on. That's why my father want to go to hiding, but my mother wouldn't go.

INT: But your, your feeling in, in April, '44, was that you were just being sent to a big factory?

LR: Just someplace to work. That's where I think we're going. Just someplace to work. And we-- they send us to-- it's near Munkács. In a brick factory they gathered us together. There was like barracks so they could put people, thousands and thousands of people from surrounding villages. And we were staying there for four weeks.

Even that time already, we saw what's going on. They used to get the men to dig ditches, in the evening to cover it back up. And they used to take bricks from one place to the other place, and then in evening put it back, just to, just to put the Jew down. We already saw what's going on.

INT: In--

LR: But--

INT: Prior to, to going to the brick factory, did your father have any discussions with you and your mother about leaving Obava?

LR: Well, like I told you, he, he didn't want to go. He heard what's going on in surrounding villages, that they picking up Jews and taking them to, to places, to gathering to places. Let's go hiding. But my mother wouldn't go. She didn't believe that something bad could happen. My father felt that something will happen.

INT: And did your-- your father was not insistent.

LR: No. She-- he didn't want to leave my mother and go. He didn't want to leave her.

INT: Did you have an opinion as to what you should do at that time?

LR: Yeah. I want to go to hiding too, but my mother wouldn't go. So--

INT: Did you plead with her to try to convince her to go?

LR: Yeah, but she, she just wouldn't go. She wouldn't go.

INT: And how old was your mother at that point in '44?

LR: In the late 40s. She still was a young woman.

INT: So the Hungarian police came to your door.

LR: Came, and they say-- it was a Sunday morning. They say get a few belonging of your clothes, belongings, and we going. There was no question as where we're going. They, they come. They just--

INT: How many men came to your house?

LR: Excuse me?

INT: How many men came to your house?

LR: Two. Two police came. Two gendarme-- it's like police-- came to our house and said, get your few belongings, clothes, and we going go, just like we were criminals. And we went. And they tooked us to the brick-- and all the people in our-- in the village was laughing. Oh, go ahead, Jews. They taking you away now.

INT: Did you--

LR: Looking and laughing.

INT: Do you remember what the policemen looked like, their faces?

LR: Well, they were tall Hungarian police. I mean, in-- they tooked us to the brick factory. From there you couldn't get out. Everything was watched.

INT: When they came into your door, were they harsh with you?

LR: They just said take a few pieces, and there was-- that's all was said.

INT: Do you remember what you took with you?

LR: Just a few-- I remember a few pieces of clothing, a pillow. I mean, how, how much could you carry?

INT: Did you take anything that was personal to you?

LR: No any-- nothing, just a few piece of clothing. That's all.

INT: No photographs, jewelry?

LR: No. Nothing, nothing. We thought we coming back. We going come back.

INT: So on a Sunday morning in April, 1944, you walked out of your farmhouse--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --that you lived in your entire life.

LR: And my grandparents lived there. My parents lived there, my grandparents' parents lived there-- and walked out.

INT: And you thought you were leaving for a few days.

LR: It's, it's only thing if they take it-- could take everything away, but they were left the family is alive. But they took it this away too.

INT: This brick factory, where-- what town was this in?

LR: It's near Munkács. It was called Podhorany. It's spelled P-O-D-H-O-R-E-N. It was, it was like 10 minutes from Munkács.

INT: And how did you get from Obava to the brick factory?

LR: We walked.

INT: How many miles was it?

LR: It took about an hour walking, so-- [INAUDIBLE] miles what took an hour walking.

INT: And this is when the townspeople were laughing and pointing.

LR: Laughing. Looking and laughing. Go ahead, Jews.

INT: Many of these people were your friends.

LR: They, they were happy to get rid of--

INT: Were your friends.

LR: We thought they were my-- they were our friends, and they were laughing when, when, when-- they were not our friends. We-- you could see who's your friends. When something happen, you haven't got no friends. The Jew didn't have no friends. We just thought we had friends before.

INT: Were there any Gentiles in Obava who acted kindly toward you?

LR: No. Nobody. Even the priest there, he-- you know, they used to come to the, to the priest. They used to come and give this-- the Jews' name where they live, to make sure that nobody was left out. They gladly give the names and where everybody lives, the name and everything, to make sure that everybody was taken.

INT: Once you arrived at the brick factory, what, what kind of living conditions did you have there?

LR: The living condition, we were living like in, in barns. You know, they were putting on, on brick floors. They were putting hundred of people together. It was terrible already. There were--

INT: But you were with--

LR: --a kitchen. There were a kitchen. They cooking and just enough to survive. It was already beating. There was already, there was already-- you could see that-- we didn't know what-- what's-- we thought they were take us to work.

INT: But you were with your mother and father.

LR: Yeah, right.

INT: And did you feel secure because you were still with your mother and father?

LR: No. Nobody felt secure there. You don't know who's-- tomorrow who's going to be here, because they used to beatings. They used to going on already there at the brick factory. The soldiers already used to beat up and shoot people.

INT: Did you ever sense-- while you were at the brick factory, did you ever sense fear in your father or mother?

LR: Yes, I did. But there's-- you couldn't get out from there already. When you were there, everything was, everything was watch-- under watch. So you couldn't get, you couldn't get out, you couldn't, from there.

INT: Did your father try to calm your fears?

LR: He did, but he was very bitter that my mother wouldn't go in hiding and he just go in the enemy's hand like this freely.

INT: What kind of food did they give you while you were at the brick yard?

LR: Just enough, enough to, to survive, a little soup there you cooked in the kitchen. It's very little food, but enough that you could survive. That's all.

INT: But you had no, you had no knowledge--

LR: No knowledge that we--

INT: --of what was going on at the camps.

LR: --they taking us. No. They-- we were all the time told they're taking us someplace for work. We never told where they really taking us.

INT: Do you remember the day that you were moved from the brick factory?

LR: Yeah. They gathered us in, in freight trains like animals.

INT: What date was that, do you remember?

LR: That was in May-- we, we were there almost four weeks at the brick factory. They gathered us in May-- was around 13, 14 of May-- to the freight, freight train. They put it-- in every, in every, in every place they put so many people you haven't got place where to sit.

And they give you a bucket you have to go to the bathroom. We had a bucket there. And when the train stopped, the man tooked the bucket and empty it. So we--

INT: Were these German--

LR: We didn't have no food. Hungarian.

INT: These were still Hungarian soldiers.

LR: Still Hungarian. And they, they-- we could hardly sit, there were so many people pushed together in that train.

INT: Did they tell you where you were going?

LR: No. We didn't know anything where we're going.

INT: What was the, the weather like when-- during--

LR: Well, it was in May. It was--

INT: Was it warm?

LR: Yes. The weather was nice. Weather was nice. But tooked us about three days.

INT: The train trip was three days?

LR: Three days, three nights we went, then we came to Auschwitz.

INT: And how many people were on a, a train car?

LR: [SIGHS] Well, we stuff few thousands of people. Because it's a long freight train.

INT: But in, in one particular car, your car.

LR: In one particular car used to be-- it was [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I would say around 200 people. There were people-- you couldn't move.

INT: If somebody fell asleep, could they, could they lay down?

LR: You could suffocate. You couldn't lay down. You couldn't lay down or you could, you could sit. You were, you were, you were kind of-- you-- they were pushed together how many you could pull-- how many you could put in in one, in one place.

INT: What, what are your recollections of the three days on the train?

LR: Was very bad. Very bad. I mean, lots of people, sick people who, who died there in the train. I mean, they were just suffocated.

INT: What happened--

LR: It was hot. Closed doors. Hot. It's a cattle train, train, so it's-- was terrible.

INT: What would happen if somebody died on the train?

LR: Nothing. When we come to the station, they took them off. The, the train stop at the station, next station, they took the dead people off. They tooked the bucket that we have to go to the bathroom and empty it, the, the people from the train, and go further.

INT: Did you have any food and water on the train?

LR: Nothing. Nothing. Nothing. Lots of time when the train used to stop and they give us water, and everybody would, would reach for the water. Nobody get anything because everybody want it.

INT: So for three days, you went without water.

LR: For three days and three nights, yeah. We didn't have nothing.

INT: Were there little children on the train?

LR: [LAUGHS] Not in our specially-- was no small children.

INT: And during the train trip, did you talk to your parents?

LR: We talked and, and we prayed. Looks when the Jew all the time believes in praying that maybe this would help, this would free us-- all the time hope for miracles.

INT: Did you ever talk to your father or mother about where they thought you were going and--

LR: Yeah. We thought we were going in-- I mean, nobody had answers. Nobody knew. Till we came to Auschwitz, then we saw where we were going.

INT: How many guards were on the train with you?

LR: Nobody was on the train. Everything was locked.

INT: Everything was locked.

LR: They locked the train. The-- was no guards. There was no windows. On the freight train there is no windows.

INT: Were there people screaming on the train at all?

LR: No. Everybody was quiet. Nobody was screaming.

INT: Did you have any friends from your hometown who were also on that train with you?

LR: Yes, yes. People that I knew. Yeah.

INT: In your car with you?

LR: Yeah.

INT: Any girlfriends?

LR: Yeah. People that I-- lots of people that I knew were all together.

INT: Did you-- were you able to talk to anybody else on the train at all?

LR: You talked. But we were so miserable you, you just want to go wherever you're going to get out of that.

INT: So after three days on the train-- did they ever stop to pick up other passengers or--

LR: No. The train was full. They loaded up full from the, from the place, and you, you didn't, you didn't stop to pick up.

INT: And it was all Czechoslovakians from your region.

LR: All, all the Carpathian, right.

INT: Do you remember what day you came to Auschwitz?

LR: Yeah. It was in May. Around the 14, 15 in May, we came to Auschwitz.

INT: Do you have a recollection of the train--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --coming to a stop?

LR: The train come to a stop and I could, I could see the flames shooting right up to-- shooting to the skies flames from, from the chimneys.

INT: What did you think those flames were?

LR: And, and the smell from human flesh. I don't know. I asked my father, what are those flames? What is this smell? I just-- you know, you-- and you could see everybody rushing, get out fast.

My father didn't answer. He, he knew what it was. I just, I just didn't knew what, what, what it was. But then fast, fast. Everybody go down from the train. They, they put us in, in-- there was staying a German SS man with white gloves, right, links, who goes right, who got left.

INT: Links is left.

LR: Links is left, right is [? recht. ?] [? Recht. ?] [? Recht ?] is right and links is left. And you have to go. He show with the finger with the white gloves. They said that was Mengele. You know, at that time who knew who he was? But they said it was Mengele, you know, telling you go right and, and-- or left. And all the old people-- the young people went one way, and the other went the other.INT: --Rosenberg, June 2, 1995. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] Lillian, as, as the doors opened on the train, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] do you remember your first images?

LR: The first-- when the doors open there was the people who were working there, the Jewish boys who were working already in Auschwitz, who came. The-- now, the Polish Jews were taken away four years before us because the war was in Poland. So they were working.

And when the people came they starting fast, fast, get out, fast, get out of the train and everybody rushing fast. We, we go and stand in a rye of-- in a line of five. And they were an SS man. They said it was Mengele, to go-- the older people and the children to, to one-- to right, and, and the young people went to left.

We didn't know why we're separated, the children screaming, running after their mother. Or the mother was young, they took-- put to the left. And it was terrible. So they, they took us in-- they tooked us to give us baths, showers.

INT: Were you next to your parents as you got off the train?

LR: We're together, but we were separated.

INT: When were you separated?

LR: Right away. Soon we, we, we came off the train and we starting to walk, they were pointing with the finger to the left, to the right, to the left, to the right. And we were separated.

INT: And where were you sent?

LR: And we, we went to a place-- they-- it's a shower place. And they give us showers. And they shaved us head and all the part-- the private bodies. Everything they shaved us. We didn't recognize each other when our hair was shaved.

INT: When you were separated from your parents, this was within a few feet of the train?

LR: A few feet of the train, right.

INT: And--

LR: The train came right into Auschwitz, right in-- into the camp.

INT: Do you remember saying anything to your parents as you were being separated?

LR: Nothing. It was no time. It were just everything so quick, so fast, so, so rushed. Everything was-- went so fast that I don't remember anything. Just at once I find myself on one side, and they are gone.

INT: Do you have any visual recollections of your parents?

LR: [SIGHS] I just-- the whole thing happened so fast that I don't know where we are going. I don't know where they went, till next day we find out where they went.

INT: And that next day--

LR: The next, the next day we, we-- you know, we-- when-- after we were giving a-- it's called like for, for disinfection bath we gave-- they gave us. And they shaved us. And they put us the striped clothes.

And we were, we were sent to the barracks. Now, we were working-- I was working in Brzezinka, which is spelled B-R-E-Z-E-N-K-A. There was in Auschwitz A, A camp, A lager, A camp. And the, the gas chambers were on one side the street. We were working in the barracks across from the gas chambers.

Our job was there when the people came, they brought there their clothing, and they all-- before they were sent to the gas chamber, they, they have to take off their clothing, have to put everything together for them when they come back they should have their clothing in a bundle. They were told they going take a shower.

INT: These were old people.

LR: They were children, everybody who-- young, old. And they have to undress outside. Lots of them didn't want to undressed. Then they, they had there the German Shepherds, the dogs. They had no choice. They undressed right there and put their clothes in bundles. Everything was packed in a bundle, to go take a shower.

There was a-- first room was showers. The next room they all pushed together, there was the, the gas chambers. And after the gas chamber was full, they was packed together. Then there was the Zyklon B gas let in, and you-- we hear the people screaming for four or five minutes, and then quiet.

And then there were young boys in the 20s-- they were called the Sonderkommando. That those were boys who used to take those bodies and take it out and put it in the, in the, in the crematorium to burn the bodies. So it was very hard work. Every three months, those boys was sent-- they got so weak they couldn't do their work anymore, so they were sent to the gas chambers too. There were about 300 boys doing that job.

Now, we were working. People came and waited because the gas chambers were full. They were sitting and asking us in Yiddish where they are taking us. We didn't answer them because we didn't had-- we didn't have what to say to them. So then they say, they don't understand Yiddish. They must be not Jewish. And we didn't have no word to say them. They-- where they taking you.

INT: That first morning--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --after you arrived in Auschwitz, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] how did you learn-- or what was the fate of your, of your parents?

LR: How do I learn what?

INT: What, what was the fate of your parents? Where were your parents taken?

LR: My parents were taken and they were, they were put to the gas chambers. They were not there anymore. So we know from the people from before us where the-- where they all went.

INT: And your-- both your parents were immediately taken--

LR: My mother. My father went to work.

INT: So your mother went immediately to the gas--

LR: Right away. But my father went to work. They told me that my father went to work, and he was working in a camp as a carpenter. And then they-- he broke his leg and they took him to the gas chambers.

INT: And when did you find out about your father's fate?

LR: After the war. The people who were with him, somebody came home and they said what's happened to my father. I know my father was a person who could do anything. He could do carpenter. He could do a shoemaker. He could do everything.

So I know if it's possible, he will survive working hard, he, he could do anything. But after he broke his leg, they not going cure you. Put him in the gas chambers.

INT: Did you have a number?

LR: For my father-- for myself?

INT: Yes.

LR: Yeah. It's A-5718, the tattoo. Everybody was tattoo who went to work.

INT: And when did they put the tattoo on you?

LR: Right the second day when we came. The tattoos-- they put the tattoos.

INT: That first full day in Auschwitz--

LR: Yeah. We-- our job was-- from the people who had all their clothes, the bundles, the, the boys used to load it up in the wagon and bring it to our barracks, and we used to sort that. We have to put everything separate. So every different articles all together, everything, like the coats separate, the pants, everything separate in bundles and tie it together, and we send this to Germany.

We have to look for if there is jewelry, lots of times in the, in the sleeves, in the pockets it was sewed in. We have to look for that and give to the Germans. Nobody put nothing away because we knew that we never get out from there. That the place that-- after everything be finished we be sent to the gas chambers too.

So there we were working. Our food was a little coffee, black coffee in the morning. And at night was a piece of bread, very little, and a little soup made, like, from grass.

INT: Were there all women in your barracks?

LR: All woman, right. Separate. All woman.

INT: And--

LR: And they also, they also put in the food-- in our food a powder that we shouldn't have our period. So nobody-- lots of people never get-- never had children.

INT: How many people were in your barracks?

LR: A few hundreds. We used to have, like, bunk beds, three, one, two, three on top. And there was nothing on the bottom, just, just wood. We used to sleep six, seven on one bunk bed.

INT: Six or seven people in a, in a single bed?

LR: Yes. Yeah. In a bed.

INT: Did you have blankets?

LR: Nothing, just the-- just wood. Nothing. Even nothing to cover up, not anything.

INT: Was there a stove or a fire?

LR: It was nothing in the, in the, in the barracks. Anything.

INT: How were you able to stay warm during the colder months?

LR: I don't know how we, how we survived. It was so cold and we didn't had anything. Used-- we keep warm one with each other closed. We were so close that we can warm one from the other, our body temperature. You know, one was-- there were-- we, we were kind of-- kept warm one, one, from the other. That's all I think they-- we could survive the winter.

INT: How close was your barracks to the crematorium?

LR: On one side street-- the, the gas chambers, the crematorium was on one side street, and we worked on the other side street, just across the street. So I would say-- [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS] well, what would you say from across street-- one street across the other street? It would be--

INT: Maybe 50 feet?

LR: Yeah. There must-- 50 feet because it's right across the street.

INT: And where was the gas chamber in relation to the crematorium?

LR: Right near-- the gas chambers and the, and-- the crematorium was on one side, the gas chambers on the other. The ovens-- the ovens was on right near the gas chambers. The-- they tell us-- the boys was telling us that the people, the bodies were so stuck together, there were so, so many people, they had a hard time to tear them apart after they were dead. There were so many people put in that, that room where they put the gas in it.

INT: Did you ever-- you, you said that you didn't tell people where they were going. Did you ever want to tell somebody?

LR: No. We couldn't. How can we tell they taking you to the gas chambers? How could you say that? I, I just-- you couldn't say it. Lots of people just couldn't take it. Now we had the electric wires surrounded our camp, and lots of them-- lots of mothers who their children were taken away, they run to those electric wires. If you touch the electric wire, you were electrocuted.

INT: And that was a preferable death to the gas chamber?

LR: They, they just couldn't take it. The mothers, lots of them just couldn't take it. I mean, it was--

INT: When you were aware of the fate of your parents--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --what were your thoughts at that point?

LR: What could you say? What could you do? I mean, you just-- I, I just can't understand because it looks to me like we become like wild, hungry animals. The people were screaming there, and we were hungry. We were looking through their, their bundles that they brought it in to our barrack to sort. We're looking for, for food. We're looking in their pockets for a little food.

So we just become like, like, like hungry, wild animals. That's how we became. There was-- the, the atmosphere was so bad that you just-- you change. You're not a human being anymore. You felt like that's it. They would-- you know, you-- tomorrow you go to the gas chambers, and it's--

INT: Did you ever cry at Auschwitz?

LR: We cried and we, we-- I mean, we did everything. We thought we don't care we die. We just had one good meal. I don't care if I die. Just one good meal, that I have enough food to eat, if I die after that. But once I should have enough food to eat. We're on talking about ourselves.

INT: Were there any people who you knew before the war who were in the barracks with you?

LR: Yeah. There were from, from our place, yeah.

INT: Did you stick together? Were you friendly?

LR: We were all, all the way till, till the end, till-- we liberated together too, from our village, two girls. They were-- we were close together. And we were liberated together.

INT: While you were in the barracks, did you help each other?

LR: There is nothing to do. There is nothing to help each-- I mean, there is nothing-- there is only talk, you know, what you would do and what you would cook. All of them talk about food. When you hungry you talk all the time about food, and-- but it's just-- we, we thought we never-- there is no way out from here.

INT: You were convinced that you were going to die at Auschwitz.

LR: No, we were convinced that there is no way out from here. You're seeing that, that beating, you know, the boys. You-- the, the SS men used to run, run, get a few boys, just to make fun. Run fast and then lay down, and they beat them. Run till they died. You-- this in-- right in front of your eyes.

It's-- a person was nothing there. A Jewish person was nothing there. It was only-- I wasn't known by my name. I was only known by my number. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Nobody called you about your name.

INT: Did your fellow Jews in the barracks call you by your name?

LR: Well, when we talk with each other. But, but by the, by the Germans we were known only as the number. We never were known as our names. Never were called our names, all the time by our number.

INT: You said you felt like an animal, dehumanized. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Can you describe how, how you acted differently than you would act today, or outside of the camp?

LR: We, like I say, were acting-- you know how, how hungry, wild animals are? That's how we acted. It was, it was un-humane. That we become-- you know, working in this circumstance, and looking and, and working in the-- in, in a place like this, how could we be-- how could be-- how could you be human?

How could you not lose your mind? How could you-- it would be-- it's impossible to be normal, to be there. I think we were un-normal. We were just become-- looking for food. You know, it's, it's-- something. You know, these people are still screaming there, and you already go through their pockets and looking for a little food.

How could we be normal? It just-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] it's impossible to be-- in this situation to become normal. I think they ruin us for our life. Because even now, you know, you, you dream that you taken away at night, and you wake up so exhausted that, that we're still there. We're still going on. I mean, they take away our-- they, they ruined our lives mentally and physically.

INT: Did you ever see people try to hurt somebody for a scrap of food, women fight over food?

LR: They would. They would. They would. Yeah. They would. I mean, you just like, like animals. You know, when they tear-- they're hungry, give them a piece of food, they will kill each other. The same thing we became.

Just-- I don't know how we come out and we still could exist. I don't know how normal we are, but we still could do things and go. And so sometimes I'm wondering myself how, how we could be still the way we are.

INT: Did you ever get used to the smell?

LR: You get used to it, everything. I mean, you haven't got no choice. That smell from human flesh was-- those flames were going straight to heaven. All the time thought-- the Jews all the time some miracle happen. When bad thing happen, some miracle. And I all the time thought was waiting from some miracle, but nothing happened.

INT: Today, could you-- if, if you close your eyes, could you smell the smell? Do you remember the smell?

LR: Oh, yeah. You, you, you still could smell. You still could see those thing. Those thing-- lots of people came from there and they removed their number. They not want everybody's asking you, what is that number on your arm?

But you could remove the number, but that's something, it stays in your heart. You cannot remove that. The number doesn't bother me. I mean, it's something, it stays with you. It's, it's not the number that worries you. It's something that's left.

INT: Are there any particular [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] incidents or acts that could happen in your daily life today that will trigger memories of Auschwitz? Anything in particular?

LR: Now would you repeat the question?

INT: Is there anything that happens in your daily life that makes you think about Auschwitz?

LR: [SIGHS] It's sometimes when you see the skinheads. Sometime you see the Nazi party, what they doing. Scares you. It scares you. I mean, it's nothing-- I mean, the-- now the, the world, especially the Jewish population, is watching closely this shouldn't happen like Hitler.

They said he's crazy. There is nothing he could do. But we find out otherwise, that one person what he did. So now when you see those skinheads and those Nazis, it's-- it bothers you. You just can't believe that, that it's happening again. You know, it's-- when you see that. It scares you.

INT: Is there any ordinary smell or sight that really triggers memories?

LR: It's, it's something stays with you, where-- I mean, it's-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] that thing will stay with you till you die.

INT: Did you have Jewish guards in your barrack? Were female prisoners guarding you?

LR: Well, guarding us-- when we marched we marched with SS men. We don't march by ourself.

INT: So--

LR: We can't, we can't get out from the camp. The camp had the electric wire, and then we had on four, on four-- we had watchtowers which guards staying in the watchtowers.

INT: So there were no guards in the barracks at night with you?

LR: No guards. You don't need no guards. You can't get off from there. If you go to the, to the, to the fence, there is electric so you get electrocuted right away. There is no out.

INT: In general, what was your recollection of how the SS treated women in Auschwitz?

LR: [SIGHS] They treat us very vicious, very vicious. I just couldn't be-- I, I couldn't believe that a person-- somebody could be so vicious. Now, when they were in Nuremberg during the trial, they will all say [GERMAN]. They all were [GERMAN]-- not guilty.

The none of them-- all of them just-- we only did the order. There was-- that we were told to do. Not true. They did on their own everything what they wanted. We couldn't complain. We didn't had to who to complain. So everybody could do what they wanted. They all were [GERMAN]. All were guilty.

INT: Were you ever beaten or abused by a guard?

LR: No. They not-- it's, it's-- like-- the guards, they kept-- they-- we were beaten by the guards. Not, not-- I myself wasn't-- was never beaten. I-- you know, it just happened that way. But--

INT: Did you see other women who were beaten--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --or abused?

LR: Yeah. Abuse, you mean sexually abused? No. The guard cannot-- oh, if a guard would touch a Jewish prisoner, that's-- that means death for him, because you-- how dare you, you touch a Jewish prisoner? They were a dirty Jew. You, you know, it's--

INT: So you weren't aware of any--

LR: No, no, no.

INT: And would these guards ever talk to you in a kind way or do anything in any way kindly to you?

LR: We had there in our barracks older guards. They were in their 50s. Because I remember the Gypsies used to be there who, who did the pavement there in the-- in Auschwitz, in Brzezinka.

And they was taking-- one night they were taken all to the gas chambers. And they were screaming because they know where they were going. They know what's going on. So the SS men who was with us, the older guy, said, oh, there's only the Gypsies. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] Because--

INT: Was that a sympathetic remark towards Jews?

LR: Yes. That's only the Gypsies. I mean, he was kind of telling us that I don't like to be here. He was an older person which was [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] sometimes said a, a friendly word to us. But mostly of them-- mostly the young guys-- the young SS, they were very, very brutal.

INT: Were they ethnic Germans?

LR: Very vicious. More-- no, was Germans. Were all Germans.

INT: Did anyone ever give you extra food?

LR: No. No.

INT: While you were at Auschwitz, what was the, the least you ever weighed there?

LR: Who weigh, who weigh-- who, who could weigh themself? We were skin and bones.

INT: Skin and bones.

LR: We were on the end in January when the Russian army came close, [SIGHS] we're just skin and bones. We-- I'm sure we weighed very little. We had lice. The lice went in in our skin. They used to-- whatever blood we have left, suck that blood out for-- you know, it's-- from dirt we got lice, everything.

INT: Was there any medical treatment available to you?

LR: Medical treatment? If you-- somebody gets sick, the medical treatment was the gas chambers. That was-- that would be the medical treatment. Who complained? They knew if you say something where you wind up.

INT: There was no camp hospital?

LR: No hospital, no anything, no anything. Now, the, the Sonderkommando-- now, those I told-- like I told you before, those-- the boys who used to work with the dead people, you know, take the people in the, in the, in the crematorium, in the ovens.

Now, every three months they used to kill them. But one September in 1944, they had ammunition, those, those, those 300 Jews strong boys. We-- they were not strong anymore after three months working. They blow up the crematorium.

INT: This was in September of '44?

LR: That was in September. And, and--

INT: This was right across the street from you.

LR: Right across the street. And they-- the-- they know that, you know, they all going die. But they knew they will die-- they were took the gas chambers. At least they going to do something. They kind of tooked revenge.

Now, we're told that three of them run away because we stand us five in Appell. Appell means five in a row and we're counted. Now, the, the, the boys-- I mean, the guards starting to shoot from the watchtowers, they were singing the 'Hatikvah.' Till the last one, they were singing the 'Hatikvah.'

INT: These were the 300 boys.

LR: The, the Hebrew, the Hebrew hymn they were singing. And you could hear the voice-- voices were going quieter and quieter, till the last one they were singing the 'Hatikvah.' Three of them that time escaped.

INT: And-- go ahead.

LR: And after that, the-- that gas chamber, they-- I mean, they were destroyed, the gas chamber across from our street. So nobody was gassed there anymore.

INT: When you were outside listening to the 'Hatikvah'--

LR: We were-- we had tears in eyes. I mean, we thought, well, they will shoot us too. We-- I mean, we-- when you come to that situation, we didn't care. They-- we gonna die. We don't have to suffer any longer. It's probably would be the best for us.

You know, we-- lots of time we thought-- because the people who were going, they said we-- if-- whoever survive, why don't you take revenge on our enemies, what they did to us. But we didn't--

INT: Did you feel pride?

LR: We didn't believe. We cried. And you know, when we-- you hear the 'Hatikvah' singing and the voices getting quieter and quieter, there-- till the last one. That takes some courage to do it. To sing the 'Hatikvah' in a situation. And you know that in, in few minutes you'll be gone.

INT: Did you feel a sense of pride that these boys were strong?

LR: Yes, we did, that they had the chutzpah, the guts, to do it. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It's-- they put us in Appell. They want to know if nobody from us run away. And--

INT: Were you aware of any other instances of young men escaping, or women escaping?

LR: No, nothing. No, no. That's only, only time that three people escaped.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

But on-- in, in January when the Russian army-- I mean the German army retreated, we saw German army coming. We could hear the shooting. We know the war is ending. But we knew there is no hope for us. I mean, the war will end.

But the last few days we use-- we were told to burn all the, the-- anything with names and, and addresses. Anything with name and address we had to burned up. They no wanted nothing to leave. We burned everything. It tooked us a few days. And then in January 15, we were stand five in a row, and we, we were marched further in Germany.

INT: How many people do you think were left in the camp January 15?

LR: Oh, no-- nobody-- from, from the-- so from, from our people nobody was left in the camp. We're all marched together. We're marching day and night. And, like, we were holding hands five together and marching like this. So we used to say now I close my eyes and you, and, and you, you know, you hold me and we walk. And we're walking, you know, for days and days in the cold.

And if you sit down, you were shot. You are so many people on, on the street. You see dead lying. You, you couldn't sit down. If you just fall down-- you were just exhausted and you fall down. Cold and exhausted, you fall down, right away you were killed by the guard. The guard were on the side, the guards with dogs.

INT: I had a question about Auschwitz. Did you ever dream while you were in Auschwitz?

LR: No I don't remember. I, I don't remember anything about dreaming when I was in Auschwitz. No. Never.

INT: Did-- was there ever a time that you had hope for a future, for a life?

LR: That we, we going come out from there? Never. I thought the-- that's, that's-- there is no way out from here. We all the time thought there is no way out from here. On the end, we were all be gassed.

INT: So there was never a point during your stay at Auschwitz that you held out any hope.

LR: No, we never-- that-- we never that we get alive from here. We never thought that we going to get alive from here. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It's, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] it's-- I don't know. It's just-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] how one, one, one, one group of people could do to another group of people like this. Just--

INT: When they lined you up for the march--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --did they tell you where you were going?

LR: Nothing. Just [GERMAN].

INT: And this was January 15, 1945?

LR: Right. 1945. We-- you know, we--

INT: And you could hear the Russian guns?

LR: We could hear the, we could hear the shooting. We hear the guns. We could see the German soldier, you know, retreating. We see-- we could hear the war is close. The war was just a few miles. The Russian were a few miles when they tooked us. Because I remember a few days later, they occupied Auschwitz, the Russian.

INT: How long did you march for?

LR: We marched for a few days till we came to another camp, Malchow. It's M-A-L-K-O-V. We were there for a while.

INT: Where-- what country was that?

LR: That's in Germany. From there we went to Ravensbrück, which spelled R-E-- R-A-V-E-N-S-B-R-C-K. We were there for a while. After that on one side the American came, and the other side-- one side Elbe, the water Elbe, the American came. On the other side, the Russian came. So we're all time marching deeper, deeper into Germany.

INT: While you were marching, what kind of clothing did you have on?

LR: What kind of-- we had still our stripe, stripe clothes.

INT: And this was in January?

LR: It was in January, right, and wooden shoes. Wooden shoes. I don't know how, how-- I don't know, I don't know how we still-- how I'm still here. I just don't believe myself that-- how I-- how a person could survive that. It's just-- I don't know.

And on the end, we was in a, in a-- sent to a German, a German farm, put there in a barn. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And there was no-- we don't see no German people there in that, in that village or little city. No, was a farm. Was a village.

And there were workers who worked for them-- not Germans, different nationality who worked for the Germans. And they come in and they said that SS man told them-- the two SS man we had-- they going take us to the forest and kill us. When they come to take you out--

INT: OK. We're--INT: Lillian Rosenberg, June 2, 1995.

LR: We were marching. So you-- they tell us to sit down and take a rest. And they were on the fields. And they would put potatoes in the ground. It was May, so the farmers plant potatoes. So we starting to dig up potato. Maybe we could eat. If they saw, they would shoot you and kill you--

INT: For-- for--

LR: --or get a piece of grass. You know, get a-- anything on the ground, with their-- so you know how-- how-- how hungry we were, that anything from green, anything-- or if you-- if you see a potato peel, they would kill you for it. An SS man would kill you for if you go get a piece of peel or a-- or a piece-- or-- or just pick a little grass and eat it.

INT: Did you stick with anyone in particular during the march? Was there a friend who you stuck with?

LR: Yeah, there-- there were two girls from our village. They were-- we were till the end. We were liberated together. And we kind of stuck together, and we were, all the time, managed just to be together.

INT: What were-- what were-- what are their names?

LR: The name were [NON-ENGLISH] [? Eva, ?] and [? Chaia, ?] which it was [? Helen. ?] [? Chaia. ?] You know, we used to call us in Hebrew names, each other, in the Old Country.

INT: And the three of you marched together?

LR: All the-- yeah, with different girls. But these were the girls from our village, which we stuck together from the-- the beginning till the end.

INT: Do you think--

LR: And we were liberated together.

INT: Do you think it helped to have people who you were friends with with you?

LR: It-- it kind of helped, because it's-- you could-- it just helped us be together.

INT: Were you ever aware of any of the local populations when you were marching?

LR: When we were marched to Germany? No. They just--

INT: How many SS guards were there for every prisoner?

LR: No, with not-- with us, with the girls I don't know. With the-- with the man, but with the-- with-- with the-- us, with the girls, they were-- they were-- I know we had two. I mean, we were about-- about, I will say, 50, 60 girls.

INT: And they had machine guns?

LR: They had guns, not machine guns. They had just regular guns.

INT: Did you ever think about trying to run?

LR: [SIGHS] No. You're afraid. I-- I mean, you were not afraid. You just-- you were just not-- oh, you know, you just-- I don't know if we know what we were doing. We were just so undernourished, so-- so hungry, so with lice, that [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] it's-- I don't know myself, like I say, how-- how I survived. After all this, how could you-- how you could survive?

INT: Where did you stop marching?

LR: Well, I can't remember the village, but it's-- we stopped marching in a-- they took us in a-- to a German village, on a farm. And they put us in a barn. And they left. The SS men left.

And on the workers who worked there in the-- in the-- with the German farmer, there were about 12 foreigners. I don't know what their nationalities were. They come in, and they tell us that these two SS men want to take you to the forest and kill you.

But when they come in to take you, say you're not going. They would be afraid to do anything, because we're 12 people here, and there are no Germans. They all run away in the forest. They were afraid for the Russian, because the Russian was so close.

And they knew the German-- Germans knew when they were in Russia what they did to the Russian, how they treated them. So they were very afraid of the Russian. They would treat them the same way.

So they all run away. They left their houses. They run away in the forest.

So when the German SS-- two SS men-- came in, and they say, [GERMAN], 'Get up; we're going further,' we all say, [GERMAN]. No, we are not going further. Everybody were shaking, why we said we are not going further And they closed the door and never returned. And on the-- the workers come in and say come in the house, and--

INT: These were gentile workers?

LR: Gentile workers, right.

INT: Why do you think they helped you?

LR: I don't know. I don't know why they-- they help us. And they-- and they told us to go and get food in the houses. There was nobody in the houses. We went, and to get food. Lots of them ate so much, there was the last-- the last supper.

INT: Of those 12 girls, how many died from eating too much?

LR: [SIGHS] No, it was about-- in that-- in that barn, we were about 50, 60 girls.

INT: And how many died from eating too much?

LR: Not-- were a-- very a few were left.

Then the Russian came. The Russian soldiers-- first time I saw a Russian soldier, and a German officer going, his hand up, and a Russian soldier behind him holding the gun and marching him, that's when I saw. And the Russian soldier come up to us and talk to us, which I speak Russian.

And he told us to come. It was about now about 50 minutes' walk, where their offices-- where they're-- where they're stationed. And they-- the doctor checked us, give us vitamins. And they give us fluid. They want to give us solid food till we get a little nourished. People-- we were very undernourished; very, very weak; very-- and there were lots of them died, because just too weak. Had lice and everything.

INT: What were your thoughts when you saw the Russian soldier with a gun, not a German?

LR: I just-- we just had tears in our eyes, that we saw that. After we liberated, we still-- we still couldn't believe there is no SS walking behind us. You-- it tooked us a long time to-- to-- a long time tooked us to-- to realize that we are free. We could walk.

But we've still-- kind of you felt that the SS man is still here for a long time. It wouldn't go away. That thought-- those thoughts wouldn't go away. The SS man is not with you anymore. You could walk.

INT: Do you see a-- a-- a particular face? Do you-- do-- do you have images of these two SS men who were on there?

LR: Yeah, you know that good-nourished SS men, with-- and we-- we-- we-- we skin and bone with-- with lice. And you-- you see those faces all the time, and you see-- you could see those-- those two faces. You never forget those faces.

INT: Were they young or old?

LR: Young.

INT: How old?

LR: They all were young.

INT: How old do you think they were?

LR: Yeah, they were, I would say, in their late 20s.

INT: Strong men?

LR: Strong men, you know. It's good-nourished. It's-- but the Russian care-- tooked very good care of us. The Russian doctors in the Russian kitchen, they tooked good care of us. So.

INT: Were any of the SS men captured and killed on the spot?

LR: Yeah. That they did.

INT: Did you see that happening?

LR: Yeah. Yeah. The Russian came. Because the Germany did the same thing when they were in Russia. They killed so many Russian-- for no reason. Come in the villages and kill them, like in Babi Yar. You know Babi Yar? They are surround-- they-- whole villages-- Jews and non-Jews too they killed. So when the Russian came, they'd invaded--

INT: When you saw--

LR: For-- for just-- they'd invaded for-- for trials. They then took them to Nuremberg.

INT: When you saw that happening, what-- what were your emotions?

LR: Well, you know, I-- I am not a violent person. I don't-- I don't like-- but at that time, you felt that they gotted what they deserved, what they did to us. I mean, nobody-- nobody could repay it, what they did to us, killing 6 millions. It's-- I don't know how somebody can-- how can somebody pay it? I mean, you know, we-- but the Russian, they--

INT: If a Russian soldier would have handed you a gun and said, to you--

LR: I myself couldn't done it. If they give-- I-- I don't-- I don't know. I didn't care if they did it. Now I couldn't-- you know, I couldn't stand shooting. Even on television, the movies, I can't stand it. But I couldn't myself, couldn't kill, even-- even they were our enemies. I couldn't do it.

INT: Did you see any other prisoners who-- who took violent acts against--

LR: They did. They did.

INT: --the SS?

LR: They did. They did. On the liberation, you know, they did. But that time, they quick tooked off their clothes, the SS men, tooked off quick their clothes and putted on regular clothes to not recognize them. But if-- if--

INT: Prisoner.

LR: --they recognized-- a prisoner recognized them.

INT: And what kind of-- what would-- what would the prisoner do if they recognized an SS man in civilian clothes?

LR: They would get rid of him fast.

INT: The day after you were liberated, you were still with-- were you with your two friends--

LR: Yes.

INT: --from Obava?

LR: Yes. Yes.

INT: And where were you sent that next day?

LR: We were there with the Russian Army, because the war was still not finished. There-- there was in-- there was in April, and the war was still going on. So we were on the-- on the north side of Elbe, the water Elbe, in Germany. We were there for four weeks. Just the Russian doctors took care of us. And--

INT: Did you stay in tents?

LR: --do nothing-- doing nothing.

INT: Did you stay in tents?

LR: No, we stayed in a-- in a house, in a German house, with the Germans, like I told, you run away in the forest. So they put us in a German house. And we were staying there.

INT: At-- at that time, what were your thoughts, in terms of, you know, trying to find family, or--

LR: Yes, well, talking, I mean, you know, that we're really free. There is no SS man walking. And like I say, it tooked us a long time to realize that we are free, that there is no SS man on the side walking with us.

[SIGHS]

But then we were-- who talking, who knows who was left. Who knows we could find it, if, you know, to go look for-- for-- for-- for somebody from the family, somebody that we know.

So we went, you know, to-- back to Germany. And then we wind out in Gabersee, which is a DP camp.

INT: How do you spell that?

LR: Is G-A-B-E-R-S-E. There it's about an hour from Munich, or near-- its near Wasserburg. Wasserburg is W-A-S-B-U-R-G. That was near Wasserburg. There was a camp for the-- after the war, the people who were saying there, we registered to come. If you have somebody in this country, in the United States, they have to send you an affidavit. So you registered. And you have to wait for your next number.

INT: When did--

LR: It tooked a few years. It tooked us there from 1940s-- '45, we came to this country in '49. So we staying there 3 and 1/2 years in Germany.

INT: When you first got to the camp--

LR: DP camp, yeah.

INT: --what-- what month was that in?

LR: We were four weeks there. It was be around-- around June. June, July we came there.

INT: And do you remember what it was like the first day in the DP camp?

LR: Well, we had enough food. And we found-- we found some people that we know, somebody, you know, from surrounding villages looking around. And they tell a story. I saw so-and-so was killed, and so-and-so was killed.

And everybody tell their story, who you were with, and who was-- do you know if somebody is-- because there were many DP camps in Germany. Besides-- besides-- besides our camp, there were a few camps. They were-- the-- the people from the camps were gathered and waiting to go to Israel, or to different countries.

So it's-- we were looking maybe to a different camp. Maybe somebody from-- from our family is there. So all of them looking for-- looking for-- for somebody-- some from the family, who's left.

INT: Did you meet anyone else from Obava in that camp?

LR: Yeah, I met my husband there. He-- he came to that camp too, which I know him from before the war, because we lived together in the same village.

INT: But you didn't-- did you date him before the war?

LR: No, I didn't date him before, no. Because I was in the-- I was living in the city at that time, going to school in Munkács. So I-- I didn't date him.

INT: But you knew him. He was--

LR: Yeah, I knew him. I knew his family. I knew his brothers and sisters.

INT: And how did you meet him in the camp?

LR: Well, he came in the same camp to wait for a visa to go to the States. And we met there and got married, his-- him and-- and his father. His father survived too.

INT: How long after you were reunited with him did you get married?

LR: We got married in '46. In--

INT: So how long did you--

LR: In June of '46, we got married.

INT: And you saw him again for the first time after the war in-- in--

LR: After the war, right.

INT: --in May of '45? Or June--

LR: I saw--

INT: --in '45?

LR: I saw him in-- I saw him when I come to Gabersee, to the camp-- DP camp.

INT: What do you think attracted you?

LR: It's-- it's-- it looks to me-- lots of people at that time got married. They didn't look-- I mean, our ages were just two years older. But lots of people, if you find somebody that you know, you were so looking for somebody, for some person from your-- from-- from the past. So you don't even look at ages.

Lots of girls got married, somebody who was 20 years, 30 years older, just somebody-- they were so lonesome for somebody. So everybody was killed from the family. So they didn't-- they didn't look, the age is nothing.

Just-- I don't know it was something wrong mentally with the people or something. I don't know. You know, it's-- they didn't look. The only thing is a Jewish person and-- and is somebody from our-- from our place. The ages doesn't matter.

It's-- you wouldn't-- you-- you were just so lonely from-- from-- from some-- some person, from-- from the same place as you come, to find somebody. Somebody you know was-- still survived. So you didn't look the ages or anything. Just-- you realize a few years later that you made the mistake. But--

INT: But you didn't make a mistake?

LR: No, no, I didn't, because my husband was-- I knew him from-- from home. I knew who he was. I mean-- and he was only two years older. But lots of them came to the State, and see that--

But mostly our people, even though married some older, they still-- they didn't believe too much in divorce. Like here in the United States, you believe in divorce. They were still lived together.

INT: Your marriage ceremony-- did a rabbi perform it?

LR: Yes, a rabbi performed it.

INT: And were you married in an orthodox ceremony?

LR: Yeah, an-- yeah, orthodox.

INT: The-- what do you remember from the ceremony itself? This was in a-- a-- a displaced persons--

LR: It was-- it-- it was like-- like a big room. They used to pray there. They made this a temple. It was-- at that camp, you know, it was big houses, or a big house. They made a temple.

That's where the chuppah was, which is Jewish, you know, chuppah for-- for the wedding. You put a tallit over. And-- and there-- it was very simple. It was, you know, a few people.

INT: Did you have a maid of honor?

LR: Yes. Yeah, we knew we have people that-- that we--

INT: Who was your maid of honor?

LR: --that-- that we knew. It was a cousin of my husband's. And it's-- it was simple. It was--

INT: Were your two friends-- your two girlfriends from Obava-- at the wedding, as well?

LR: No. The-- the-- those two girls went to-- to Israel. They didn't come to States.

Now I couldn't come, either, because I didn't have anybody here. My husband had here an uncle and a aunt which came before the war, in their 20s. And you have to have somebody here who could send you an affidavit, guarantee that you wouldn't be a burden on the country. And they guarantee you-- they guaranteed that you-- they-- they will support you, in case you don't work.

So my husband's uncle and aunt, they sent us an affidavit. And that's why I could have come. If I went by myself, I couldn't come to this country. Be-- without no family I would have to go to Israel-- which wouldn't be that bad, either. But only you have to have somebody here to come to this country.

INT: When did you first find out about your brothers?

LR: [SIGHS] I find out about-- about-- about my brothers when I were in this country. They wrote to me. No, somebody-- somebody who-- went home. And-- and they give them my address, who went to the Carpathian. And they give them my address, and they give me their address. And I used to send them packages.

But about 10 years ago, they died, one after the other. One year, and the other died the other year. They got sick. They came sick home from the-- from the war.

INT: So you didn't find out about your brothers while you were in the displaced person camp?

LR: No. Till-- till I was already in this-- in the United States.

INT: So you had assumed that they were dead.

LR: They were dead. Lots of people-- it happened that the people got married. They thought their husband was dead. And they-- they found out that her husband was alive. Those thing-- not many, but it did happen.

I mean, you didn't know. You-- it's-- you didn't know who is alive, who is dead. Even you wait so long.

But it happened in our-- in-- in-- in-- near our village, is that the girl got married, and her husband came home-- the first husband. So she had to divorce both of them, according to Jewish law, and marry whoever she wanted. But she divorced both of them and didn't married either one.

INT: Did your brothers move back near your-- your hometown?

LR: Yes, they did.

INT: In Obava?

LR: In Obava. Right.

INT: And they lived their whole lives in Obava?

LR: They lived there, yeah. One of them-- one of them lived in [NON-ENGLISH]. It's a big city.

INT: Did they ever visit you in the United States?

LR: No, they never came to the States. They never came here.

INT: And you-- and you never saw them.

LR: No, I never saw them, just some pictures; never in person. I never saw them.

INT: So the last time you saw your brothers--

LR: Was in '44. And-- no, they left before. I didn't-- I see them in '42. '42, '43 I saw them last.

INT: Did you ever want to see them again?

LR: I just felt I couldn't go back home. I just-- I just couldn't-- couldn't go back.

Like, my son now, who lives in Philadelphia, he wanted me to go to Auschwitz with him. He didn't want to go himself.

But I feel that I just couldn't-- I just couldn't put my-- I couldn't step in that ground anymore. It feels like-- I just couldn't do it.

It just-- I couldn't do it. I couldn't-- I can't do it. I can't go there. And it just would be too much.

He go. He begged me a few times, why didn't you come with me to Auschwitz? I would love to see it.

But lots of people did go, you know. The people-- the parents went with them. But I feel I couldn't do it, so I didn't go.

INT: How many years did you spend in the displaced persons camp?

LR: From-- one year.

INT: Pardon?

LR: In the-- in-- in Auschwitz.

INT: No, in the displaced persons camp. The DP--

LR: On DP camp? Well, from-- till '49. We came August 9, '49, to the States.

INT: So it was four full years.

LR: Yeah, we waiting. Because you register, and you're waiting for your next number-- till your next number comes. So we're waiting there a long time.

INT: And when did you first get your affidavit?

LR: Well, we get affidavit before we came. If you get affidavit right away, they-- you got-- you got your shots, to make sure you have all the-- all the shots for-- to come to the States. And in August 9, we came to the--

INT: But what year did you get the affidavit, or the-- the-- what year did-- did your--

LR: Well, it-- it would be in '49.

INT: In '49.

LR: '49.

INT: So your husband's aunt and uncle didn't send the letter until '49?

LR: No, no, no. The-- he send the-- he send the papers right away. But when you come to the camp, you register. And when your number comes next, you have to wait till your number comes up next, to wait.

INT: So how long did you wait?

LR: So-- in 3 and 1/2 years--

INT: Three and a half years.

LR: We were-- we were there in the camp and waiting, because it's-- it goes according-- when you're registered to come.

INT: Was it very frustrating for you to wait such a long time?

LR: And the-- and the food wasn't bad there. It was only not much to do. Not much to do. You had work in the kitchen. The-- and it's-- the-- the HIAS used to send us clothing.

So it's-- it was kind of-- we were wondering where we go and settle. But at least we had a hope that we come to the United States, and we start our life at new.

I don't know. You live in hope. You live the hope that-- I don't know. It took even 3 and 1/2 years, but, you know, one day you're going-- you're going to leave Germany, the German ground.

INT: Your friends who went to Israel--

LR: They went to Israel.

INT: --have you ever had contact with them?

LR: No. No. We kind of separated, and now-- and never had contact with them.

INT: Have you ever been to Israel?

LR: I was. I was. About six years ago, I was in Israel.

INT: But you never looked to either one of them, or--

LR: No, I couldn't find them. I don't know their addresses, anything.

INT: How many children do you have?

LR: Two.

INT: And when were they born?

LR: My son [? Sammy ?] was born in January 30, 1951. And my daughter, Marilyn, was born in August 28 in 1954.

INT: And in 1949, where did you first come to in America?

LR: We come to Ben Harbor, Michigan, which my uncle-- my husband's uncle lived there. We were staying there. And on my aunt lived in St. Paul, Minnesota. We went there. She find a job for my husband and myself. We were there for a short time.

And then we came to Gary, Indiana. My father-in-law got married there. And he told-- he wanted us to come to Gary, Indiana.

And in Gary, Indiana, we opened our furniture store, my husband and I. And we had a furniture store. And in '68, we had a holdup, which my husband was killed in a holdup. Right before my eyes he was shot. After you-- you go through four years hell, you come to this country just to be killed for a few dollars.

INT: This was in 1968?

LR: 1968.

INT: And you were right next to him?

LR: In February 22, 1968. [SIGHS] We-- when they killed him, and they run out, and I run next door to the bank-- next door to my-- to our furniture store was a bank-- they should call police. And they came back. They say, why don't you go-- go help your husband? You know, he's dying.

And I just went to the bank. And the police came in no time. And then I have to-- the-- I have to testify, go to court, to make sure they were sentenced-- they will get jail sentences. Because if I wouldn't testify, they wouldn't.

It was very, very hard for me to go. But my rabbi insisted that it's your duty. And I felt too that I don't care.

Lots of people was threatening me. You going and testifying. Aren't you afraid for your own life? You have children.

And I told them, I-- I feel I-- I have to do that. I have to do that. They cannot walk around freely after what they did. So they were-- I went to court a few times, testified, and pointed out who they were. And they put them-- I think one of them got life in prison.

INT: What was your husband's name?

LR: Bernard. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] He was still only in the 40s. It was in '68. 40-- yeah, 40-- 48, 49. He was 49 years old.

INT: So you had two children at that point.

LR: Yeah I have my-- yeah, my daughter was 13 at that time. My son was 16. They were all-- both in high school.

And after that's happen, I still stayed in the store. It was very hard. Lots of people came in, and reminding you, feeling sorry for you. But I have to raise my children. So I stayed in the store for the-- for another 17 years, till my children finished college, got married.

And about 10 years ago, I give up the store. It was come-- very bad area there. So I closed up the store. My children was all the time afraid something happened to you. Why don't you give up the store? I give up the store and retired. And now--

INT: And you were in the store by yourself?

LR: Yeah, I had, you know, somebody help me. But I run it by myself after my husband passed away.

INT: Did-- were you frightened by the prospect of having to run this business by yourself?

LR: No, I was-- I knew about business, because I was staying with my husband together when-- when he was alive. So I know everything about business. The problem was, it was a little too much.

But you have to do it. I mean it's-- it's nothing that business was new to me. Since I was together with him, I knew everything about the business. So it's the only thing.

It was harder by yourself than together. But you have to do it for your children, you know. They were-- I wanted them to go to college. I wanted them to-- to make something of their lives. I mean, I didn't care-- I could have got married. I couldn't care.

I just wanted my children to be-- finish college, and-- and get a good education. And when you have good education, then you-- you-- you're OK here in this country. So that's all I did. I didn't care about myself.

INT: Did you try to shelter your children from your pain?

LR: I-- no, I-- I wanted my children just go to school. Just go to school. I didn't want them to stay-- like my son, stay with me in the store. I said, no, you go to school. I will take care of the store myself. You just go to school, and get an education; get a profession.

INT: And what does your son do today?

LR: He's a CPA. He own his own business, and his wife is also a CPA. They have two children, which does very good, thank god. I'm happy for that.

That what keeps me going, is my children, my grandchildren. If not them, I don't know if-- if not them, I don't know if-- if-- would be really hard. Would be really hard to exist.

But they're so nice to me, my grandchildren, my children. So that keeps me going. It keeps me alive.

INT: When your husband was killed--

LR: Yeah.

INT: --did you find that you were thinking about your experience during the war?

LR: It's-- it just-- you just-- you feel like you couldn't believe it, that-- but that's what it was. And there's nothing you could change.

INT: Did you ask yourself--

LR: You ask yourself, why? But who-- who has the answers? That nobody has the answers.

INT: And what about your belief in God? How did the incidents--

LR: Yeah, I believe in God. But I was-- like I say, I was waiting for miracles. And-- and-- no, I was very religious before the war. Like, I was orthodox.

I'm still-- you know, my house is kosher. I can't eat unkosher meat. I would never eat it. You know, it's something you grow up. You just-- you just can't change. I can't change. I mean, maybe somebody else.

But I just-- you kind of feel, why nothing happen? Why didn't some miracle happen? It's all the time in-- in Jewish history, you've-- you hear this happened, this happened with the Jews-- miracles. Why no miracle happened that time? Why-- something could had happened that this shouldn't happen to us. But nothing happened.

INT: Do you believe in miracles?

LR: Yes, I do believe it happened. But it didn't happen, so-- but you know, in our past history, you knew a miracle happened, didn't it? So--

INT: So you still believe in miracles.

LR: I-- I still believe. I still believe, even if it didn't happen to me.

INT: Have you tried to make any sense out of what happened to your husband? Have you--

LR: I mean, what-- what could you make it? I mean, what-- what-- he's gone. And I-- and I-- I have to try my best, how hard it was. But I have to try my best, and try whatever I could do. So it's-- that's all you could do, is try.

